<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=rapid-transit">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rapid transit</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Metro, Subway, Underground</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rapid transit is high-capacity urban public transport that runs on an exclusive right-of-way, usually on electric railways. The world's first rapid transit system was the partially underground Metropolitan Railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, which opened in 1863 using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-locomotive">steam locomotives</a> and now forms part of the London Underground. In 1890 the City &amp; South London Railway became the first electric-traction rapid transit railway and the first fully underground one, which brought the word subway into railway usage. The 1893 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> Overhead Railway was designed for electric traction from the start.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rapid-transit.jpg" alt="Rapid transit" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JoachimKohler-HB · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U9_im_U-Bahnhof_Zoologischer_Garten.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
